# NativeAOT and trimming

Release builds are NativeAOT; development runs on the JIT (design §0.1). Both must behave the same, so library code is written for NativeAOT first.

- Every project under `src/` is `IsAotCompatible`. The trim, AOT, and single-file analyzers run in every build, and their warnings are errors.
- No reflection that the trimmer cannot see: `Type.GetType(string)`, `Activator.CreateInstance(Type)`, `MakeGenericType`, `MakeGenericMethod`, `GetMethod`/`GetProperty` by name, `System.Reflection.Emit`, `Expression.Compile`. Code that needs a type-driven table is generated by a Source Generator (`XylemUI.Generators`) or written by hand.
- No `[DynamicallyAccessedMembers]`, `[RequiresUnreferencedCode]`, `[RequiresDynamicCode]`, or `[UnconditionalSuppressMessage]` to silence an analyzer. If one seems needed, the design is wrong for AOT; raise it on the Issue.
- Serialization, if any, uses source-generated serializers (System.Text.Json source generation or similar), never reflection-based ones.
- Interop uses `LibraryImport` and unmanaged function pointers, not `DllImport` with runtime marshalling or `ComImport` RCWs (`.cursor/rules/interop.mdc`).
- Generic virtual methods and generic interfaces instantiated over many value types grow the binary. When one is added on a public type, the PR reports the size of the NativeAOT sample before and after.
- A Source Generator ships inside the package that uses it (`analyzers/dotnet/cs`), not as a separate package (MagicOnion's layout).

## The check

A PR that adds or changes a `src/` project's public surface, a dependency, or interop runs a NativeAOT publish of the sample its Issue names:

```powershell
$env:PATH += ';C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft Visual Studio\Installer'
dotnet publish samples/<Sample> -c Release -r win-x64 -o <scratch>
<scratch>/<Sample>.exe --auto
```

Under `dotnet run`, a project with `PublishAot` reports `RuntimeFeature.IsDynamicCodeSupported` and `IsDynamicCodeCompiled` as false too. To tell the builds apart at run time, use `System.Runtime.JitInfo.GetCompiledMethodCount() > 0` (JIT).

The PR's `## Verification` states the warning count (zero) and the size of the executable.

A size that grew is split by what added it before the PR says so: publish with `-p:IlcGenerateMapFile=true` and compare the map files. In F1 the map showed that source-generated COM linked the reflection runtime (+0.43 MB, replaced by a hand-written vtable), and that most of `HelloWindow`'s growth was `Process` (about 286 KB) and LINQ (about 20 KB), added only to print figures (PR #152 review). A sample whose size is reported gets the feature it shows and nothing else; figures that need more code are measured in `benchmarks/XylemUI.E2E`.
